Package: plugin-db
Section: utils
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: plugin database: pin, show and verify plugins
 The command plugin-db pins an entry of the plugin database from the plugin's
 files, shows one entry's attributes, state and reason, and verifies every
 entry as rpm -V does, with the JSON Schema of the store's layer files. It
 loads no plugin.
 .
 Its documentation is on openmixer.org.

Package: plugin-db-dev
Architecture: any
Depends: libssl-dev, ${misc:Depends}
Description: plugin database: headers, static library and pkg-config file
 <plugin-db/pin.h>, header-only, on libc and libcrypto: the SHA-256 of a file,
 the omx-layout/1 fingerprint of a parameter layout, the words a pin is written
 with and the rules of what is a pin's. <plugin-db/db.h> and libplugin-db.a:
 the address form, the file collection with the LV2 bundle rule, the store of
 layers and the state function. With the pkg-config file plugin-db.
 .
 Its documentation is on openmixer.org.

Package: plugin-db-contract
Section: libs
Architecture: all
Multi-Arch: foreign
Depends: omx-contract (>= 3.18.0~), omx-contract (<< 4~), ${misc:Depends}
Description: words of the plugin database in omx-contract's contract directory
 data/pin.json of plugin-db, the words of a pin, and data/db.json, the address
 formats, the bundle rule, the layers, the attributes, the record changes and
 their actions, the states and the reasons, as the package plugin-db of
 omx-contract's contract directory, under
 /usr/share/omx-contract/contract.d/3/plugin-db.
 .
 Its documentation is on openmixer.org.

Package: python3-plugin-db
Section: python
Architecture: all
Depends: plugin-db (>= ${source:Version}), plugin-db (<< 0.5~), plugin-db-contract (= ${source:Version}),
         omx-contract-data (>= 3.18.0~), omx-contract-data (<< 4~), ${python3:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: plugin database: reader for Python
 The Python module plugin_db: query() gives the answer of plugin-db query
 --json for a plugin of the store, read() is the Python twin of the reader for
 layers in hand, and text() gives the sentence of a word from the catalog of a
 language. Its words are generated from data/, and the fixtures of the C reader
 and of the TypeScript twin hold it to the same answers.
 .
 Its documentation is on openmixer.org.

